Currently we are looping over the context fields and add all of them with their respective value to as URL parameters. `searchParams.append(..)` will gladly convert `null` and `undefined` and convert these into string values, which is kind of surprising. 

```js
const urlWithQuery = new URL("https://getunleash.io");

// Using null
urlWithQuery.searchParams.append('a', null);
urlWithQuery.searchParams.has('a');
> true
urlWithQuery.searchParams.get('a');
> "null"

// using undefined
urlWithQuery.searchParams.append('b', undefined);
urlWithQuery.searchParams.has('b');
> true
urlWithQuery.searchParams.get('b');
> "undefined"
```

this PR adds filtering of context fields which has value `null` or `undefined`.

Fixes https://github.com/elastic/kibana/issues/112782

This PR switches the approach to filter the field list to use the field caps API with an index filter instead of sampling documents. There's an advanced setting (`lens:useFieldExistenceSampling`) to switch back to the old approach using sampling.

It's touching the epoch millis test because it includes a check for empty/available fields which doesn't work anymore with just a single index, so it's splitting up the index into two.

## Sampling

The old approach is loading 500 documents of the given index pattern using the given filters and checks the documents whether they contain data for all of the fields. If there is at least one document containing data, it's marked as "available". This can lead to false negatives - a field might be shown as negative even though it has data in the current time range / filter context but by chance it wasn't part of the 500 loaded documents. This is especially likely when working with a large number of documents. However false positives are impossible in this case - a field which is shown as "available" definitely has data in the current time range / filter context.

Scripted and runtime fields are behaving like regular fields in this approach - they might show as available or empty depending on whether they have data for the 500 sampled documents.

## Index filter on field caps API

The new approach leverages the mapping data to exclude the mappings on indices which can never match the current query. Fields which are only occuring in these excluded indices are shown as "empty", the others are shown as "available". There is no chance for false negatives - this means a field is never shown as "empty" if it does in fact contain data. There is however a chance for false positives - a field might be shown as "available" (because it's in the mapping), but it doesn't have any data in the current time range / filter context.

**Scripted and runtime fields are always showing as "available" in this approach - as the scripts are not executed there is no way to know whether they would yield any data**

The mapping-level filtering doesn't work for all queries, but in these two key cases:
* constant keyword fields which have different fixed values per index and the filter is filtering out a value. Note: This does only work for the `term` query at the moment, not the `match_phrase` query which is generated by the filter UI using the "is" operator. There is an open issue for this on ES side here: https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/82515
* selected time range - if an index does not contain data in a given time range, it's excluded from the response as well

## To test

Create indices
```
PUT existence_index_1
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "filter_field": {
        "type": "constant_keyword",
        "value": "a"
      },
      "textfield1": {
        "type": "keyword"
      },
      "ts": {
        "type": "date"
      }
    }
  }
}

PUT existence_index_1/_doc/1
{
  "filter_field": "a",
  "textfield1": "test",
  "ts": "2021-01-02"
}

PUT existence_index_2
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "filter_field": {
        "type": "constant_keyword",
        "value": "b"
      },
      "textfield2": {
        "type": "keyword"
      },
      "ts": {
        "type": "date"
      }
    }
  }
}

PUT existence_index_2/_doc/1
{
  "filter_field": "b",
  "textfield2": "test",
  "ts": "2022-01-02"
}
```

Configure data view for `existence_index_*`, then go to Lens. Changing the time range to only the year 2022, "textfield1" will become "empty", changing the time range to only the year 2021, "textfield2" will become "empty".

Configuring a filter using a term query on the "filter_field" (need to use custom DSL for that), will make textfield1 respectively textfield2 empty depending on whether the filter is set to match "a" or "b".

Switching back to sampling mode using the advanced setting, empty/available should work in the same way even if the regular filter UI using the "is" operator is used.

See fix #7320

Add the support of template type on return type and parameter of a method declared using `@method` annotation, on class or interface : 

```php
<?php
/**
 * @template T
 * @method T get()
 * @method void set(T $value)
 */
class Container
{
    public function __call(string $name, array $args) {}
}

class A {}
function foo(A $a): void {}

/** @var Container<A> $container */
$container = new Container();
$container->set(new A());
foo($container->get());
```

Current version do not process template types, so calling those pseudo methods will always result to InvalidArgument error.

Fixes a crash found by [the operator fuzzer](https://github.com/cncf/cncf-fuzzing/blob/main/projects/argo/operator_fuzzer.go) where the type assertion of `m["type"]` and `m["status"]` would crash Argo because either was `nil`.

